<Site title="Privacy policy">
  <p>Last updated: 6 November 2026.</p>

  <p>
    Justus is a photo-sharing app that works directly between devices. This policy explains what
    happens to your data. The short version: AndersCan, who publishes Justus, runs no server that
    receives your photos or your data, and collects nothing about you.
  </p>

  <h2>Who we are</h2>
  <p>
    Justus is published by AndersCan. Contact:
    <a href="mailto:cankodeas@gmail.com">cankodeas@gmail.com</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>What we collect</h2>
  <p>
    Nothing. Justus has no accounts, no sign-in, no analytics, no crash reporting, no advertising
    and no trackers. We do not receive your photos, your album names, your contacts or any
    information about how you use the app.
  </p>

  <h2>What stays on your device</h2>
  <p>
    Your photos, albums, the keys that protect them and the app's settings are stored on your
    device, in the app's private storage. Grouping photos by the people in them is computed on your
    device. No photo or face data is sent to a cloud service for this.
  </p>

  <h2>What goes to other people</h2>
  <p>
    Sharing is the purpose of the app. When you add a photo to an album, it is sent encrypted,
    directly from your device to the devices of the people in that album. The album's owner
    approves each person who joins, and a share link on its own does not show anyone your photos.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Everyone in an album can see its photos and who else is in it, and can keep their own copy.
      Once a photo has reached another person's device, we cannot take it back from them.
    </li>
    <li>
      The photos' details (such as the name you give them and when they were added) travel with
      them. Anything stored inside a photo file, such as the time or location a camera recorded,
      goes with the file. Check your photos before you share them.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Network and your IP address</h2>
  <p>
    To find the other people in an album, the app uses a peer-to-peer network (a distributed hash
    table, DHT). Devices in that network, including the people you share with, can see the IP
    address of your connection while you are connected. We do not run this network and do not log
    these connections. If this matters to you, use the app over a VPN.
  </p>

  <h2>Permissions</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Internet and network state:</strong> to connect to the people in your albums. The app
      does not ask for access to your whole photo library, your location, your contacts or your
      microphone. You choose each photo you add.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Third parties</h2>
  <p>
    Justus sends data to no third-party service. Google Play and your phone's operating system may
    collect their own data under their own policies.
  </p>

  <h2>Deleting your data</h2>
  <p>
    Remove an album or a photo in the app, or uninstall the app, to delete what is stored on your
    device. Copies that other people in an album already hold stay on their devices, and you can
    ask them to delete them. Because we hold no data, we have nothing to delete on our side.
  </p>

  <h2>Children</h2>
  <p>Justus is for people aged 18 and over. We do not knowingly collect data from children.</p>

  <h2>Changes</h2>
  <p>
    If this policy changes, we will update the date at the top. The page's history is public in the
    <a href="https://github.com/AndersCan/justus">Justus repository</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Contact</h2>
  <p>
    Questions or requests: <a href="mailto:cankodeas@gmail.com">cankodeas@gmail.com</a>.
  </p>
</Site>
